cartridge "CHOPPER PILOT/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1987"

' CHOPPER PILOT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Chopper Pilot" by Chris Boss, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette disk of October 1987 (Commodore 64, compiled machine code started
' by SYS 2420; the author's name is in the program: ",,, BY CHRIS BOSS ,,,").
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's, read from its
' machine code (orig/dis.txt) and watched in a C64 emulator (orig/*.mjs);
' the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision: rolling country that scrolls
' smoothly by half tiles, a 32 x 16 sprite helicopter, an instrument panel,
' sound.
'
' The game: fly a helicopter low over country that streams past from the
' left. The button is the throttle: hold it to speed up, let go to slow
' down. Pull the stick back to climb, push it forward to dive (PILOT; the
' title also offers ARCADE: up climbs). Each column of country you pass
' scores by your clearance over the ground under the cabin: under 8 pixels
' 5 points, under 16 4, under 24 2, under 36 1. On the flat stretches stand
' flags, radio masts and trees, and birds fly over them: touch down on a
' flag (clearance under 3) to take it, 100 points; brush it higher up and
' it falls (no penalty). Hitting a mast, a tree or the birds, or passing a
' column while you touch the ground, is an ERROR and costs 25 points (only
' while the score is 256 or more, as on the C64). A level ends when you have
' flown its miles (30.00 on level 1, 20.00 after), when its clock runs out
' (180, 140, 150, 160, 170 seconds) or at 25 errors; then you go on only if
' your score has reached the level's bonus mark (10000, 14000, 17000,
' 20000 here; the C64 asked 18000, 25000, 31000, 36000). Level 5 is the
' last. At 20 errors the sky turns yellow.
'
' From the original (orig/dis.txt): the land is a column of height 0-20
' half rows; each new column keeps its height, rises or falls (one chance
' in three each) by the level's step (1, 2, 3, 5, 7; a fall smaller than
' the step goes down by 1), capped at 20; at height 0 it stays flat for 20
' more columns, then rises. On a flat column: a flag 32 times in 256, a
' mast 8, a tree 8, birds 16 (in the 6th or 9th of the 13 rows), else
' nothing. Throttle 0-26 (shown as speed 0-104), changed by one every 8, 8,
' 6, 4, 2 passes; altitude 0-83 pixels, one a pass. Measured in jsc64: at
' full throttle the C64 passed about 25 columns and climbed about 25
' pixels a second.
'
' Liberties: the bonus marks are 5/9 of the C64's (its 18000 on level 1
' needed about 90% of the most a level can give: 3000 columns at 5 points
' plus every flag; a human-paced bot made about 11000); the speed grows in a straight line with the throttle (26
' columns a second at 104; the C64 moved one column every 27 - throttle
' passes, so its middle speeds were much slower than shown); the throttle
' steps every 18, 18, 13, 9, 4 frames, the climb is 1/2, 1/2, 1/2, 5/8, 3/4
' pixel a frame (the C64's passes got faster on later levels); errors are
' counted per column passed while touching the ground (as on the C64) and
' once per mast, tree or birds hit (the C64 counted those only if the hit
' fell on a column step); touches by boxes, not pixels; flags, masts,
' trees and birds never stand on the first or last column of a flat
' stretch nor side by side (so they scroll by half tiles); each level has
' its own land colours (the C64 striped the flat columns in the level's two
' colours); the sky colour is chosen on the title (the C64 let the stick
' pick it on the level screen); the ARCADE stick option; sounds: a rotor
' chop that quickens with the throttle (the C64 played an engine tone),
' flag, crash and level jingles, a title tune; a title page and credits;
' the end goes back to the title (the C64 asked AGAIN Y/N).
'
' Controls: joystick (either controller): stick back/forward climbs and
' dives (PILOT) or up/down climbs and dives (ARCADE); a button is the
' throttle. On the title, up and down choose, left and right change, a
' button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

